[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The JWT token shall be transmitted only over encrypted channels using Transport Layer Security (TLS) to prevent interception and unauthorized disclosure of sensitive information.', 'rationale': 'As per the source, information must be transmitted over encrypted channels to prevent disclosure to unintended parties.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to authenticate via JWT in an OAuth 2.0 flow;
WHEN the connection uses TLS;
THEN the JWT token is not visible to intermediaries or unauthorized parties.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'In cases where sensitive information must not be disclosed to the client, the JWT shall be encrypted to the authorization server.', 'rationale': 'The source specifies that JWTs should be encrypted to the authorization server when preventing disclosure of certain information is desired.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to authenticate with a sensitive identity;
WHEN the system determines that disclosure to the client must be prevented;
THEN the JWT is encrypted and sent only to the authorization server.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall include only the minimum amount of information necessary to complete the authentication exchange in the JWT token.', 'rationale': 'The source emphasizes that deployments should determine the minimal information needed and include only such claims in the JWT.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user requests authentication;
WHEN the system evaluates required claims;
THEN only essential claims (e.g., 'sub') are included in the JWT, and no extraneous data is transmitted."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The 'sub' (subject) claim in the JWT may represent an anonymous or pseudonymous user, as defined in Section 6.3.1 of RFC 7521.", 'rationale': "The source references the OAuth Assertion Framework [RFC7521] for the use of 'sub' as a value for anonymous or pseudonymous users.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user with no real identity;
WHEN the system generates a JWT;
THEN the 'sub' claim shall contain a pseudonymous identifier, not a real user identifier."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All database queries that use user-supplied input must use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.', 'rationale': 'SQL Injection is a critical vulnerability that allows attackers to execute arbitrary SQL commands. Parameterized queries separate SQL logic from user input, ensuring that input is treated as data, not executable code.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user submits a product name via a search form;
WHEN the application constructs a database query using that input;
THEN the query must use parameterized placeholders (e.g., '?' or ':name') and the input must be bound to the query as a parameter, not concatenated into the SQL string."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': 'The application shall allow users to search for products by name and return a list of matching product details.', 'rationale': 'The core functionality of the application is to provide a product search feature to users.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user enters a product name in the search field;
WHEN the user clicks 'Search';
THEN the application shall retrieve and display product details that match the input name."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'All database queries must be executed on the server side, and no client-side query construction is permitted.', 'rationale': 'Client-side query construction using string concatenation is a known vector for SQL injection attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to submit a search query via JavaScript or client-side code;
THEN the query must not be executed on the client; instead, it must be sent to the server and processed there.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'no-store' directive for any response containing sensitive user data, such as passwords or personal identification information.", 'rationale': 'Prevents sensitive information from being cached and exposed to unauthorized users via shared caches or browser private caches.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a user profile containing personal data;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: no-store'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'max-age' directive to specify the freshness lifetime of cached responses for static content, such as images and CSS files, with a value of 3600 seconds (1 hour).", 'rationale': 'Enables efficient reuse of static content without requiring validation, improving performance and reducing server load.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a static image file;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: max-age=3600'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'must-revalidate' directive for any response that requires validation before reuse, such as dynamic content generated per user session.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that stale content is not served without validation, maintaining data consistency and integrity for time-sensitive content.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a user-specific dynamic page;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: must-revalidate'."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall not cache responses to authenticated requests unless explicitly authorized by a 'public' or 'private' cache directive, and only if the response is marked as 'public' in the Cache-Control header.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized access to sensitive content through cache reuse by other users or intermediaries.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a protected resource with authentication;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: private' or 'Cache-Control: no-cache' if not publicly accessible."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall implement validation mechanisms using the 'Age' and 'Expires' headers to detect stale responses and trigger validation requests to the origin server when necessary.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that cached content remains valid and up-to-date, reducing the risk of serving outdated or compromised data.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a cached response that has exceeded its freshness lifetime;
WHEN a client requests the same resource;
THEN the server shall send a validation request to the origin server using the 'If-None-Match' or 'If-Modified-Since' headers."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall not allow responses to be cached when the origin differs from the request origin, as defined in Section 4.4 of the HTTP specification.', 'rationale': 'Prevents cross-origin data exposure and ensures origin-based integrity of responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request with a different origin than the response origin;
WHEN the API processes the response;
THEN the response shall not be cached or made available to clients via intermediary caches.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The API shall use the Cache-Control header field to specify cache directives, including max-age, must-revalidate, and no-cache, to ensure proper freshness and invalidation of responses.', 'rationale': 'Standard HTTP cache directives ensure interoperability and allow clients and intermediaries to manage response freshness appropriately.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a max-age directive of 300 seconds;
WHEN a client requests the same resource;
THEN the client shall use the response if it is within the freshness lifetime and not expired.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The API shall not generate quoted forms of cache directives (e.g., 'max-age') in response headers unless required by an unknown extension, and shall ensure that consumers can parse token and quoted-string forms for known extensions.", 'rationale': 'Avoids interoperability issues caused by quoted forms of directives as specified in Section 5.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a cache directive;
WHEN the directive is in token form;
THEN the response shall not include a quoted form, and clients shall be able to parse both token and quoted-string forms for unknown extensions.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The API shall not allow responses to be marked as 'public' or 'private' in a way that enables reuse under any condition, as defined in Section 5.2.2.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unintended reuse of sensitive or private data in cache systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a private cache directive;
WHEN the response is cached;
THEN the cached response shall not be made available to any client, including those with different origins or permissions.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The CI/CD pipeline shall not allow the exfiltration of secrets (e.g., API keys, tokens) through logs, artifacts, or external endpoints.', 'rationale': 'Secrets exfiltration via remote code execution can lead to unauthorized access to external services and compromise the entire supply chain.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a workflow that stores or uses a secret in a log or artifact;
WHEN the workflow is executed;
THEN the secret shall not be printed to logs or included in artifacts, and no external endpoints shall be called to transmit the secret.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The GITHUB_TOKEN used in workflows shall be scoped to 'Read repository contents and packages' by default, and any additional permissions shall be explicitly granted in the workflow file.", 'rationale': "A 'write' permission on the GITHUB_TOKEN enables attackers to modify repository contents, create or alter releases, and interact with other GitHub resources, increasing the attack surface.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a workflow using GITHUB_TOKEN;
WHEN the workflow is executed;
THEN the token shall have only 'Read repository contents and packages' permissions unless explicitly granted in the workflow file with a clear justification."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All GitHub Actions workflows shall be scanned for vulnerabilities using CodeQL, with scans configured to run on every pull request and marked as a required status check before merging.', 'rationale': 'CodeQL enables detection of vulnerabilities in GitHub Actions workflows, and requiring scans before merges ensures that security issues are caught early in the development lifecycle.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pull request to a repository;
WHEN the workflow is triggered;
THEN CodeQL shall scan the workflow file and the result shall be a required status check before merging, and high or critical issues shall block the merge.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Workflows that trigger on pull requests from external contributors (forks) shall not execute automatically unless explicitly approved by a repository administrator.', 'rationale': 'External contributors may submit legitimate-looking pull requests that later contain malicious changes, which can be executed in CI without further approval.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pull request from a non-member or fork;
WHEN the workflow is triggered;
THEN the workflow shall not execute unless a repository administrator has explicitly approved the request.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The pipeline shall prevent denial-of-service attacks by limiting resource consumption (e.g., CPU, memory, API calls) in workflows, especially those interacting with external services like LLMs.', 'rationale': 'Uncontrolled resource consumption can lead to financial loss and service disruption, particularly in paid external services.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a workflow that calls an external service (e.g., LLM);
WHEN the workflow is triggered with repeated or large inputs;
THEN the execution shall be throttled or rejected to prevent excessive resource usage and financial impact.'}, {'id': 'SEC-006', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All workflows shall be subject to periodic vulnerability scanning (at least daily) and findings shall be tracked and remediated over time.', 'rationale': 'Regular scanning ensures that new vulnerabilities are detected and addressed, reducing the risk of long-term exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a repository with active workflows;
WHEN a vulnerability scan is performed on a scheduled basis;
THEN findings shall be recorded in a centralized issue tracker and remediation shall be completed within a defined timeframe (e.g., 30 days).'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All sensitive data, including API keys, passwords, and tokens, must be masked in workflow logs using `::add-mask::{value}` when used in strings or variables.', 'rationale': 'Masking prevents sensitive data from being printed in logs, reducing the risk of exposure during debugging or log review.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a workflow step that outputs a string containing a sensitive value;
WHEN the string is logged;
THEN the value is masked in the output log using `::add-mask::{value}`.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Secret scanning must be implemented in both pre-commit and pull request stages to detect and prevent the accidental exposure of sensitive data in repository code.', 'rationale': 'Early detection of secrets in code prevents them from being committed or merged into the repository, reducing the attack surface.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a developer commits code with a potential secret;
WHEN the pre-commit hook runs;
THEN the commit is blocked if a secret is detected.
AND GIVEN a pull request is opened;
WHEN secret scanning is triggered;
THEN the pull request is blocked and a remediation message is provided if a secret is found.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The `actions/checkout` step must be configured with `persist-credentials: false` unless explicitly required for Git operations.', 'rationale': 'Preventing credential persistence reduces the risk of exposure if the workflow environment is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a workflow that uses `actions/checkout`;
WHEN the workflow runs;
THEN Git credentials are not persisted in the workflow environment.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall detect application-layer DoS attacks, such as slow HTTP attacks, by monitoring HTTP request patterns and identifying unusually slow or fragmented requests.', 'rationale': 'Slow HTTP attacks exhaust server resources by keeping connections open until data is fully delivered, leading to resource exhaustion and service unavailability. Detection is critical to prevent resource depletion.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a slow HTTP attack is launched against the web application;
WHEN the system monitors incoming HTTP requests;
THEN it shall detect requests with unusually low data transfer rates and trigger a mitigation response within 5 seconds.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The system shall detect and mitigate MAC flooding attacks by monitoring switch MAC table usage and triggering a fail-over mode when the table is exceeded by 90% of capacity.', 'rationale': 'MAC flooding attacks consume switch memory, leading to a fail-over state where all traffic is forwarded to all ports, causing data leakage and network disruption. Early detection prevents full network collapse.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a MAC flooding attack is initiated;
WHEN the switch MAC table reaches 90% capacity;
THEN the system shall detect the anomaly and trigger a fail-over mode within 3 seconds, and log the event with a severity level of 'Critical'."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The system shall detect ARP poisoning attacks by identifying spoofed ARP messages and blocking them using packet filtering technology.', 'rationale': 'ARP poisoning allows attackers to intercept or block traffic intended for legitimate devices, leading to denial of service on local network segments. Packet filtering can prevent such attacks from propagating.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a spoofed ARP message is sent to a legitimate device;
WHEN the system inspects incoming packets;
THEN it shall identify the spoofed message and block it, preventing it from reaching the target device.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall maintain at least 99.9% availability during normal operations and under DoS attack conditions, with a maximum degradation of 10% in response time during an attack.', 'rationale': 'Availability is a core component of the CIA triad. The system must remain functional under attack conditions to ensure service continuity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DoS attack is active;
WHEN the system is under sustained attack;
THEN the service availability shall remain above 99.9%, and response time shall not exceed 100% of baseline performance.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The router shall identify and categorize legitimate control plane traffic based on known protocols and traffic patterns (e.g., OSPF, BGP, SNMP, NETCONF) to ensure only authorized traffic reaches the control plane.', 'rationale': 'Legitimate control plane traffic is essential for routing and management functions; identifying it enables effective filtering of unwanted traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router with known control plane traffic patterns;
WHEN traffic is analyzed against a defined set of legitimate protocols;
THEN traffic matching the patterns shall be allowed to reach the control plane, and all other traffic shall be either blocked or rate-limited.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The router shall deploy a forwarding-plane filter that prevents or rate-limits traffic not specifically identified as legitimate control plane traffic, with a maximum rate limit of 100 packets per second for non-legitimate traffic.', 'rationale': 'The forwarding plane is closer to the line rate hardware, making it more effective for DoS mitigation and reducing the risk of control plane overload.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-traffic network environment;
WHEN a packet is received that does not match a legitimate control plane traffic pattern;
THEN the packet shall be rate-limited to no more than 100 packets per second, or blocked, depending on configuration.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'requirement': 'The router shall maintain a logging mechanism that records all traffic filtering decisions, including blocked or rate-limited packets, with timestamps and source IP addresses.', 'rationale': 'Logging enables visibility into filtering behavior and supports incident response and auditability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a filtering event;
WHEN a packet is blocked or rate-limited;
THEN a log entry shall be generated with the source IP, destination IP, timestamp, and reason for filtering.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-ERR-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall reject a token request that includes an asymmetric key the Resource Server cannot process, using a response code equivalent to CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and including the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' as defined in Table 3.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that clients using unsupported key formats are rejected early, preventing potential cryptographic failures or security vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client submits an asymmetric key not supported by the Resource Server;
WHEN the Authorization Server processes the request;
THEN it shall respond with a CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and include the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' in the CBOR payload."}, {'id': 'SEC-ERR-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall reject a token request when the client and Resource Server do not share a common profile, using a response code equivalent to CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and including the error code 'incompatible_ace_profiles' as defined in Table 3.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized or misconfigured token exchanges by enforcing profile compatibility between client and server.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client and Resource Server have no shared profile;
WHEN the Authorization Server receives a token request;
THEN it shall respond with a CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and include the error code 'incompatible_ace_profiles' in the CBOR payload."}, {'id': 'FUNC-GRANT-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall map grant types to CBOR abbreviations as defined in Table 4 when using CBOR encoding, using the values: password=0, authorization_code=1, client_credentials=2, refresh_token=3.', 'rationale': 'Enables compact and efficient encoding of grant types in constrained environments, aligning with standard practices in OAuth 2.0.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a token request with a grant type parameter;
WHEN the request is encoded in CBOR;
THEN the grant type shall be represented using the corresponding integer value from Table 4.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-TOKEN-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The system shall use the 'pop' value as the default for the token_type parameter in CBOR-encoded responses, and shall only provide other token types if registered in the IANA OAuth Access Token Types registry.", 'rationale': 'Ensures consistency and interoperability with the PoP token model defined in this framework.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a token request with no specified token_type;
WHEN the system generates a response;
THEN the token_type parameter shall default to 'pop' in the CBOR-encoded response."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall register the 'ace_profile' parameter in the IANA OAuth Parameters registry with the parameter name and usage location defined in Section 8.9 of RFC 9200.", 'rationale': 'Ensures interoperability with other OAuth 2.0 implementations that support ACE profiles as defined in RFC 9200.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an OAuth token response with the 'ace_profile' parameter;
WHEN the parameter is present and valid;
THEN the value must be a valid ACE profile name as registered in the IANA ACE Profile registry and must conform to the CBOR mapping in Section 8.8."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall register the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' in the IANA OAuth Extensions Error Registry with the usage location and protocol extension defined in Section 5.8.3 of RFC 9200.", 'rationale': 'Provides a standardized error response for cases where the client uses an unsupported POP key, enabling consistent error handling across systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client request with an unsupported POP key;
WHEN the server processes the request;
THEN it shall return a token error response with the error code 'unsupported_pop_key'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support the PoP token type in the OAuth Access Token Types registry with the response parameters cnf and rs_cnf as defined in RFC 9200 and RFC 9201.', 'rationale': 'Enables secure token exchange using PoP (Proof of Possession) as defined in the ACE-OAuth profile.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requesting a PoP token;
WHEN the server issues a PoP token;
THEN the token must include the cnf and rs_cnf parameters as specified in RFC 9201 and RFC 9200.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall assign CBOR values for all registered parameters and error codes according to the IANA registration policies defined in RFC 8126, with values in the range -256 to 255 designated as Standards Action.', 'rationale': 'Ensures alignment with IANA standards and promotes interoperability with existing systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a parameter or error code registration;
WHEN the value is assigned;
THEN the value must fall within the appropriate range as defined in RFC 8126 (e.g., -256 to 255 for Standards Action).'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The router shall rate-limit the generation of ICMP Destination Unreachable messages by the control plane to prevent DoS amplification and ensure operational stability.', 'rationale': 'Uncontrolled generation of ICMP messages can be exploited in DoS attacks, especially when messages are sent in large volumes to trigger responses from other systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router under normal operation with legitimate traffic;
WHEN a DoS attack attempts to flood the control plane with ICMP messages;
THEN the router shall generate no more than 100 ICMP messages per second, and the rate limiter shall be activated only when traffic exceeds this threshold.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "The router shall apply different rate limits for distinct ICMP Destination Unreachable codes (e.g., 'fragmentation needed and DF set', 'Packet Too Big') to allow for differentiated traffic handling.", 'rationale': 'Different ICMP error types correspond to different network conditions; treating them separately allows for more granular and accurate traffic management.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a router receiving packets with a 'fragmentation needed and DF set' error;
WHEN the router processes such packets;
THEN the rate limit for this error type shall be set to 50 messages per second, and the system shall generate a monitoring alert when this limit is reached."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The router shall rate-limit TTL/hop limit expired packets before forwarding them to the control plane component that generates ICMP errors.', 'rationale': 'TTL-expired packets may be sent to the control plane as part of a DoS attack; limiting their processing prevents resource exhaustion in the control plane.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a flood of TTL-expired packets;
WHEN the router receives such packets;
THEN no more than 100 such packets per second shall be processed by the control plane, and the router shall generate an alert when the limit is reached.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'requirement': 'The router shall distribute the generation of ICMP error messages to Line Card CPUs to prevent overloading the control plane component.', 'rationale': 'Distributing error message generation reduces the risk of the control plane being overwhelmed during high-volume attacks or normal traffic spikes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high volume of TTL-expired packets;
WHEN the router processes them;
THEN at least 75% of the ICMP error messages shall be distributed to Line Card CPUs, and the control plane shall not generate more than 50 messages per second.'}]
